It's hard to say just what they're applying.  It looks like they're just
covering their asses ("may be subject"), rather than stating any
particular policy.

I would agree that leaving it up in the air like this, however, can only
lead to trouble in the future.  (And it's certainly untrue that "all" of
their records are subject to such disclosure.)


>Student privacy is protected by the ECPA, the FERPA, and the 4th Amendment
>of the U.S. Constitution.

While I don't think we have enought o go on regarding fundamental
protections of the Fourth Amendment, I doubt that either ECPA or FERPA
will prove terribly useful in protecting against this sort of thing.  As I
recall, ECPA only applies to systems where there is a reasonable expectation
of privacy.  I'd say that posting an explicit disclaimer, such as the one
above, undercuts any argument that a reasonable expectation of privacy exists.
As for FERPA, it applies to educational records, and it's not clear to me
that it would apply to these kind of computer records.


