Net-Nanny haters, 


These utility based services are limited.  The only way to effectively  
contextualize information search and retrieval for children or any other 
group is to provide a server based solution such as Quality Computer's LINQ.

I am currently the president of TeleDynamics, a Peoria based Internet 
Provider scheduled to deliver LINQ (filtered access) to 32 area schools 
in 16 school districts.  LINQ contextualizes the Internet (soon the WWW) 
for K-12 curriculum.

OK, now let's think about this.  Is it censorship?  I do not think so...  
Think about the precadents involved.  A Tennessee community (part of it 
anyway) sued a CA provider (of erotica) for violating community standards of 
dceency.  

The problem here is the community did not have the tools to define their 
standards own standards or view of the net.  If such tools were widely 
available I believe the judge may have ruled in favor of the net as the 
primary community and the Tennessee group as the secondary community 
responsible for their individual view of the net's content.

The K-12 access dilema deserves sensitivity from both sides of the 
fence.  The question is how do we reconcile views without stepping on any 
one person/group's rights. 

Everyone should have the right to define their own view of the net and 
their children's view as well.....  In fact the responsibility must fall 
with individual parents and schools or we will be left in the dark ages 
of centralized control. 

The net is a dynamic environment and 
decentralization means we each have the power to define our own standards 
rather than letting others define them for us.  If you want the right to 
freedom of expression then you should consider the importance of tools for 
individuals rather than all encompassing standards like the 
Communications Decency Act.


