There is no question that the prime time network TV could certainly benefit
from any infusion of quality programming. But censorship is not the
solution--if anything it is a major part of the problem.

Don't forget that censorship has been so overt that the networks even had
people with those titles. While one can joke about some of their escapades,
such as showing Elvis only from the waist up on the Ed Sullivan show, most
of the damage they have done is far from a joking matter.

The government played a major role, such as the FCC penalties against
stations and talent, for such things as their relatively benign practice of
the First Ammendment. But such governmental misconduct pales compared with
the censorship practices, including blacklists, which destroyed the
professional life, and sometimes even the lives, of many tallented, capable,
and responsible individuals. These subversive forces included the aptly
named House Unamerican Activities Committee, as well as the late junior
Senator from Wisconsin. Quite a diverse group became their victims, Charlie
Chaplin and Pete Seeger being just two examples.

HUAC didn't do this alone, they had help from a private organizations. While
all of this was done under the guise of noble and extra-legal motives, the
stench of opportunism and profiteering was pervasive. By eliminating many of
the brightest and most qualified talent from TV and the cinema, their
replacements often aquired their positions for reasons quite greatly at odds
with the requirements of quality entertainment.

With many of the best and the brightest gone, we lost the benefit of their
creativity and skills. The result, sadly, speaks for itself.

Let's not let the mistakes of the past repeat themselves, with all too
predictable results. For starters we need to make a sharp distinction
between people who quite reasonably want the freedom to choose what they and
their families see, in contrast with those whose goal is to force their
views and perjudices on others.
