Message-ID: <pete.802017645@extro>
Date: Thu, 1 Jun 1995 14:40:45 GMT

The following is from private email, reproduced with permission. The
attribution has been filed off by request of the correspondant. With a
bit of luck this might help whoever ends up doing the FAQ - and before
Paul Feonic feels totally lumbered, I'd like to nominate Murray
Chapman, author of the Blade Runner FAQ among others, as an experienced
alternate if Paul doesn't want the job.

--

The Shadow writes,

>      Here are some questions which might go into a FAQ.

Thanks! As soon as someone pops up to maintain one, I'll forward them.
For now, here's my comments.

>Q: Do I have the right to be an "Internet participant"?
>
>In some places you don't, due to repressive politics.
>In other places access may be limited due to telecom
>monopolies. 

Sounds good, though I'd substitute "commercial" for "telecom".

>Q: Should we have a right to set up our own access
>by leasing a line and plugging in a few modems?

Right 0.10 suggests that you should be subject only to the laws that
apply at your physical location - so if these laws forbid Internet
access, you have to change them, move or break the law. This last
option may cause you to incur severe penalties at the hands of various
authorities, so be careful.

>Q: "None of these responsibilities can be nor should be guaranteed
>   by law, ..."
>Many laws impose limitations on our rights
>to communicate, publish and "broadcast":
>Are these guarantees of responsibilities?
>Do they conflict with the ERR?

Let's take them by case:

>slander
>fraud

The ERR suggests that you pursue whatever legal remedies you can for 
damages you incur in these ways IF AND ONLY IF you can't get satisfaction
by conversational means - talking the problem out, possibly with someone
impartial to help moderate things.

>harassment or threats

While the ERR suggests that you have an ethical responsibility not to
harrass or threaten others (1.3), it also suggests that legal
guarantees against such actions are unethical. This is because of the
very high risk of abuse of such guarantees. Messages are easily and
convincingly forged. Irony can easily be misunderstood. Fiction and
fact are easily confused. 

Yet it can be very distressing to be threatened or stalked, especially
in a medium where anonymity is so easily maintained. Mail filters, MUD
gag-lists and USENET kill-files can fix most such problems, but they're
not perfect. Laws should permit the thorough investigation of such
incidents, but it's not an easy tradeoff to make - civil liberty for
civil safety. The ERR may err on the side of liberty - if you think so,
please feel very welcome to suggest alternatives.

>shouting "FIRE!"
>damage by misinformation

The ERR suggests (1.5) that it is your responsibility to include adequate
warning with information that may endanger others. However, legal liability
for this would result in people curtailing vast amounts of useful information
on the grounds that they might be forced to indemnify it (ie. stock prices,
software reviews, ...)

>planning or inciting criminal acts

The position of the ERR here is based on the idea that governments and
corporations are more likely to commit criminal acts than are individuals. 
The history of the world contains so many incidents of loss of life,
violation of trust and general brutality and destruction committed by
centralised organisations that incidents committed by criminal individuals 
pale into insignificance beside them. 

The ERR can not ethically differentiate between acts that are criminal
and acts that are legal, because very commonly it will hold that the
laws being broken or upheld are not themselves ethical.  The ERR
therefore would empower individuals at the expense of the risk that
they may abuse their powers. The alternative would be to accept the
rule of law as the foundation of ethics; such an alternative is
equivalent to totalitarianism.

>hate propaganda

Hate propaganda functions most effectively in a medium where freedom of
speech is suppressed. The ERR promotes freedom of expression, and thereby 
opposes hate propaganda.

That may seem to have been a rhetorical statement, but whenever and however 
bigotry has raised its head on the Internet, it has been slapped down hard,
not by the rule of law, but by the moral fibre of the many free-thinking
Internet participants who will not hesitate to flame, mail-bomb, castigate
and otherwise scorn bigots. Without gurarantees on the freedom of expression,
these free-thinkers will themselves be in danger of legal repurcussions for
their actions.

>obscenity

The ERR does not seem to recognise obscenity, blasphemy, heresy or any of 
the other victimless crimes.

>campaigning and giving election results on voting day

The ERR does not recommend against secret information. In fact, it is
all for secret information, on the grounds that individuals should be
able to define their own privacy by restricting access to the information 
that they originate or receive.

>restrictions on encryption

The ERR explicitly opposes such restrictions (0.3)

>Q: "The right of every person to restrict access to any information..."
>
>      Courts can order us to produce information as evidence,
>even in civil lawsuits. The tax people can demand information
>with little or no due process.
>Do these laws conflict with the ERR?

ERR would define such orders and demands as unethical; in practice they
might really become impracticable if encrypted cash ever became
commonplace.  But if you are able to convince the tax-man that he
shouldn't audit you because that would be unethical, then you're doing
better than you have any right to expect [...  you know, the more of
these I answer, the more I understand just how cyber-radical the ERR
really is. I had been thinking of it as a nice reasonable
middle-of-the-road kind of document ... but I can't think of how
to change it to allow the taxman to snoop ...]

>Q: "The right of access to any publicly available information..."
>"The right to distribute in electronic form any publicly
>available report, policy, regulation or law."
>
>Does this include academic publications?

Yes, except where they fall under copyright.

>Does this mean that copyrights should not be used to restrict
>these materials to paper?

No, it means that copyrights should be used to restrict access to
the materials, whatever their form.

>If I were king, which laws or policies would I have to
>change to comply with this?

Depends on just whose legal system you start out with. The ERR doesn't
give you a way to construct such a system - it just tries to give a
standard by which to judge how well it turns out. 

>Q: "The right to accept any information from any source."
>
>As I understand it, "receive" was changed to "accept" because
>some people rely on other sites to pass on information and
>these sites should not be obliged to provide free services.

The wording with receive could have been interpreted to mean "the
right to demand to receive", so it was changed to be passive - to 
accept is to consent to receive.

>This allows any site or transmission facility (many state-
>owned) to arbitrarily block your reception.

Yes.

>      Nowadays many people purchase bandwidth in the
>same way they buy phone or postal services. Shouldn't
>they have the right to "receive"?

My ISP went down last night, and I missed out on my on-line gameshow
where I would have won a million dollars - see, here are my 
lucky numbers. Now I am going to sue ...

My ISP went down last night, and so the Japanese pulled out of the deal.
We're ruined. We're going bankrupt. We're going to sue ...

My ISP went down last night, while I was being teleoperated on by the
great surgeon, Doctor Christian Barnanard. Now, I have a chronic pain
in my guts and I cannot work. I'm going to sue ...

>Q: "The right to publish any information in any public forum..."
>
>If a "public forum" is one in which anyone has the right to
>publish then this is tautologous.

No, it's not. It says that in a public forum you can publish _any_
information ... which right actually ought to be curtailed by copyright
come to think of it. This is the one that says you can post bomb 
recipes, drug advertisements and porn to the K-12 groups without fear of
prosecution ... 

>What is a "public forum"? Is an elected assembly a public forum?

Nope, it's a moderated forum. There's a chairman or a speaker and he
says what can be expressed and what can't.

>Q: "The right to seek legal recourse for damages ..."
>
>Does "legal recourse" mean a civil lawsuit or can it include
>criminal prosecution?

Yes.

>Q: "The right to ignore ..."
>
>What's this about?

Lots of people ask this. Basically it means you don't have to expose yourself
to anything you don't want to see. So when big brother insists that you
learn the Bible, the Koran and the Lao Tse you can tell him where to get off.
It also has to do with the right to use the aforementioned kill-files,
mail filters and gag-lists.

