>I just did a full text search for "expectation" in the law. I only
>see it mentioned with respect to oral communications.
>     ftp://ftp.eff.org/pub/CAF/law/ecpa.1986

I just looked for it, and didn't find it.  So my recollection was obviously
faulty.  What I did find, however, that may have been what I was thinking
of, was the fact that certain provisions only apply to providers to the public
(such as the prohibition on random monitoring) and the fact that interception
is not unlawful given the prior consent of one party to the communication.

That raises the question of whether posting a disclaimer, such as the UW-
Milwaukee one at hand, (and perhaps even making the disclaimer part of a use
agreement) can create a situation where use of the system constitutes
implicit consent to such interception.  If it can, then email interception
would not be unlawful under ECPA.

It seems like we discussed this very issue, quite some time back, with
regard to the CERT key-stroke-monitoring warning.


